{
 "schema_version": 1,
 "description": "Device sign-in (RFC 8628) one step at a time, for a caller that runs its own loop -- a Home Assistant config flow shows the code, then polls on its own schedule. begin sends POST /v1/auth/device/authorize with the scopes, the client name and the client_id given -- client_id names a registered app (Home Assistant's is thalovant-home-assistant) and is left out when there is none -- and exposes user_code, verification_uri, verification_uri_complete (null when absent), interval (seconds) and expires_in; a verification URL that is not http(s), has no host, or carries credentials is an error. An empty scope list is left out of the request, exactly as no scopes are: the API requires at least one and answers [] with a 422, and leaving the field out asks for its default. poll sends one POST /v1/auth/device/token {device_code}: 400 authorization_pending is 'pending' with the interval to wait; 400 slow_down is 'pending' with the interval five seconds longer, and the lengthening sticks for later polls of the same code; 400 expired_token is 'expired'; 400 access_denied is 'denied'; 2xx is 'approved' with the token's type, scopes, expires_at and token_id, and the SDK keeps the token (and its id) for later calls; a 2xx without an access_token, or whose body is not a JSON object, is 'error' with status null (the API did not refuse; its answer was unusable), and any other failure is 'error' carrying the api-errors fields -- status, code, detail -- as a failed begin does. describe sends GET /v1/auth/device/codes/{user_code} as the signed-in approver and exposes scopes, client_name, client_id, client_verified and device_name (absent is null, client_verified false): client_verified is true only for a registered app, whose client_name is then the platform's own name for it and device_name what the device called itself. revoke sends DELETE /v1/auth/api-tokens/{token_id} for the token the SDK signed in with; a token may always revoke itself. Revoking the token in use is idempotent: a token already revoked (or expired) cannot authenticate its own revoke, so a 401 there is 'revoked' too, and either way the SDK forgets the token and its id. The device code and the access token never appear in an error message.",
 "source": "thalovant-api app/routes/auth_device.py (authorize, token), app/routes/auth_local.py revoke_api_token; RFC 8628 §3.4-3.5",
 "home_assistant_scopes": [
  "hubs:read",
  "clients:read",
  "clients:write"
 ],
 "home_assistant_client_id": "thalovant-home-assistant",
 "message_excludes": [
  "dc-5f0c2d4e8a614c1f9d2b3e7a9c0b1f24",
  "tvt_synthetic_device_token_do_not_log"
 ],
 "cases": [
